<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=stained-glass">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stained glass</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 675</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stained glass is coloured glass, as a material or an art, made into windows from small pieces joined by lead strips called cames and held in a rigid frame. Coloured glass was made in ancient times, but the earliest known reference to stained-glass windows in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> dates from 675 CE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a> brought workmen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to glaze the monastery of St Peter at Monkwearmouth. Hundreds of pieces of coloured glass and lead from the late 7th century have been found there and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a>. In the Romanesque and early Gothic period, from about 950 to 1240, large windows were supported by iron frames, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stained-glass.jpg" alt="Stained glass" width="330" height="553" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PtrQs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chartres_RosetteNord_121_DSC08241.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1699 a major redecoration in the Louis XIV style began, and in 1756 the canons judged the interior too dark and had the medieval <b>stained glass</b>, except the roses, replaced with plain white panes.</blockquote><a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Gutzon Borglum then redesigned the torch with <b>stained glass</b>, and floodlights were installed.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>stained glass</b> of the parish church of Roquemaure in France was restored, the local priest, Maurice Gilles, asked Placide Cappeau to write a new text for the coming Christmas Midnight Mass.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-holy-night">O Holy Night</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>675</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Monkwearmouth, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Islamic glassmakers were inspired and active from the 8th century, and an Assyrian formula for coloured glass from Nineveh dates to the 7th century BCE; neither is dated as windows.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/glassmaking">Glassmaking<span>c. 2500 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=stained-glass">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
